  * sends an IPI to every core whose deadline has passed and re-arms the
  * frame for the rest.
  *
+ * bluecpu_idle=3 (0212): OS-initiated mode, as Linux runs the SDM845
+ * (cpuidle-psci-domain). PSCI_SET_SUSPEND_MODE(1) at start; cores ask for
+ * 0x40000004 (LITTLE/BIG_CPU_SLEEP_0), and the core that finds all others
+ * already down asks for 0x4100c244 (CLUSTER_SLEEP_0, the APSS and the apps
+ * RSC) when the earliest deadline of all of them is at least
+ * bluecpu_idle_cluster_us away. If another core is in fact up, the firmware
+ * answers DENIED and the core goes on as after WFI. In that state the
+ * cores' timers and the broadcast frame cannot wake the APSS (SPI 6 is not
+ * a PDC wake input), so before asking, the core writes the earliest
+ * deadline, capped at bluecpu_idle_max_ms (100), into the apps RSC's
+ * CONTROL TCS (DRV2 +0x40 lo, +0x38 hi | valid), as rpmh_rsc_write_next_wakeup.
+ * bluecpu_idle_ss (with bluecpu_idle=3): the apps RSC's sleep and wake
+ * sets, which the firmware sends when the APSS goes down and comes back
+ * (Linux rpmh_flush). Bit 1: the BCMs MC0, SH0, MM0-3 at 0 in sleep (the
+ * apps' DDR and multimedia NoC votes drop); bit 2: xo.lvl 0 in sleep (the
+ * apps stop holding CXO). The wake set puts back the last active value
+ * written to each address (blueline_rpmh_write reports every vote here),
+ * or a safe default (BCMs at their top, xo.lvl 3) until there is one. Bit
+ * 4: cx.lvl and mx.lvl at level index 0 in sleep (Linux rpmhpd's sleep vote
+ * with no consumer), and in wake at the first cmd-db level >= NOM (256). The
+ * sets are written once at start (sleep TCS 2, wake TCS 5) and a wake slot
+ * is rewritten in place when its address is voted again.
+ *
+ * The sleep set is armed (its CMD_ENABLE) only while the panel sleeps
+ * (blue_bw_park, 0211): the display engine reads DDR over the MM NoC and
+ * its DSI PLL runs from CXO, so in Linux those votes stay in sleep while
+ * the display is on. With the sleep set armed, the last core does not ask
+ * for the cluster state while a UFS command is in flight (the controller
+ * DMAs to DDR while its thread waits). ss1/ss2: armed with the panel on,
+ * the phone hung within ~25 s.
+ *
  * Every 10 s: "BLUELINE IDLE: 10s:" with suspends, returns, resumes,
  * broadcast interrupts and IPIs, and the time each core spent powered down.
